from (values
('127.414.d.10.G',3,'Small vertical surfaces at the end of a wing, called winglets, reduce drag by weakening:','["gravity", "cabin pressure", "wingtip vortices", "engine noise"]'::jsonb,2,'Winglets reduce induced drag.','original'),
('127.414.d.10.G',4,'A smoother aircraft surface reduces drag because it lowers:','["lift", "thrust", "weight", "skin friction"]'::jsonb,3,'Smooth surfaces reduce friction drag.','original'),
('127.414.d.11.A',3,'To find the center of gravity of an aircraft, engineers sum the moments of each weight about a reference and divide by the:','["total weight", "number of parts", "total length", "speed"]'::jsonb,0,'CG = sum of moments / total weight.','original'),
('127.414.d.11.A',4,'A 100 N pilot sits 2 m from a reference and a 300 N engine sits 1 m from it. The CG from the reference is:','["1.5 m", "1.25 m", "3 m", "0.75 m"]'::jsonb,1,'(100x2 + 300x1) / 400 = 1.25.','original'),
('127.414.d.11.B',3,'For an initial estimate, the center of pressure of a wing is often placed near a fraction of the chord measured from the:','["wingtip", "fuselage", "leading edge", "trailing edge"]'::jsonb,2,'Early estimates use a chord fraction.','original'),
('127.414.d.11.B',4,'The center of pressure on a typical wing moves forward as the angle of attack:','["is zero", "is constant", "is negative only", "increases"]'::jsonb,3,'CP shifts with angle of attack.','original'),
('127.414.d.11.C',3,'Static margin is the distance between the center of gravity and a reference point, expressed as a percentage of the:','["mean chord", "weight", "speed", "span"]'::jsonb,0,'Margin is normalized by chord.','original'),
('127.414.d.11.C',4,'A positive static margin generally indicates the aircraft will tend to return to its original attitude when:','["painted", "disturbed", "parked", "refueled"]'::jsonb,1,'Positive margin means static stability.','original'),
('127.414.d.11.D',3,'One way to improve static stability is to move the center of gravity:','["outside the aircraft", "aft", "forward", "to the wingtip"]'::jsonb,2,'A forward CG increases stability.','original'),
('127.414.d.11.D',4,'Another way to improve static stability is to increase the size of the:','["logo", "cabin windows", "paint layers", "horizontal tail"]'::jsonb,3,'A bigger tail adds restoring moment.','original'),
('127.414.d.11.E',3,'After changing the CG location, the static margin should be:','["recalculated", "guessed", "hidden", "ignored"]'::jsonb,0,'Changes require new calculations.','original'),
('127.414.d.11.E',4,'Revised margin calculations should be documented so reviewers can verify that stability:','["is random", "still meets the requirement", "is optional", "is irrelevant"]'::jsonb,1,'Documentation supports review.','original'),
('127.414.d.12.A',3,'A propulsion choice for a high-speed jet airliner would most likely be a:','["piston engine", "rubber band", "turbofan engine", "sail"]'::jsonb,2,'Turbofans suit high-speed cruise.','original'),
('127.414.d.12.A',4,'For a small, low-cost trainer aircraft with modest speed, a suitable propulsion solution is a:','["ramjet", "rocket engine", "nuclear engine", "piston engine with propeller"]'::jsonb,3,'Piston engines suit light aircraft.','original'),
('127.414.d.12.B',3,'Choosing twin engines instead of one can add redundancy and affect the aircraft''s:','["safety and cost", "logo", "name", "color"]'::jsonb,0,'Engine count affects safety and cost.','original'),
('127.414.d.12.B',4,'A mission that requires the aircraft to continue flying after an engine failure drives the choice of:','["no engine", "multiple engines", "a single engine", "a larger window"]'::jsonb,1,'Redundancy supports the mission.','original'),
('127.414.d.12.C',3,'The total propulsion weight includes the engine, propeller or fan, and the:','["paint", "seats", "fuel system", "carpet"]'::jsonb,2,'System weight includes accessories.','original'),
('127.414.d.12.C',4,'Two engines each weigh 150 kg and the associated systems weigh 40 kg total. The propulsion weight is:','["190 kg", "300 kg", "150 kg", "340 kg"]'::jsonb,3,'2 x 150 + 40 = 340.','original'),
('127.414.d.13.A',3,'A component that must resist high temperature, such as a turbine blade, requires a material with high:','["heat resistance", "transparency", "density", "softness"]'::jsonb,0,'Material requirements follow the environment.','original'),
('127.414.d.13.A',4,'A wing skin that must be light and strong might use a:','["cloth tent", "composite or aluminum alloy", "glass pane", "lead sheet"]'::jsonb,1,'Materials meet strength-to-weight needs.','original'),
('127.414.d.13.B',3,'A materials justification document states the chosen material, the alternatives, and the:','["paint plan", "owner''s name", "reasons for the choice", "lunch menu"]'::jsonb,2,'Justification supports decisions.','original'),
('127.414.d.13.B',4,'A good justification for choosing titanium over steel might cite its higher:','["density", "softness", "color", "strength-to-weight ratio"]'::jsonb,3,'Titanium is strong and light.','original'),
('127.414.d.14.A',3,'A fuselage that must carry cabin pressure and be light often uses a:','["semi-monocoque structure", "tent", "wooden crate", "solid block"]'::jsonb,0,'Semi-monocoque balances strength and weight.','original'),
('127.414.d.14.A',4,'An aircraft that must be easy and cheap to repair in the field may use a:','["monolithic block", "truss structure", "solid billet", "ceramic shell"]'::jsonb,1,'Trusses are simple.','original'),
('127.414.d.14.B',3,'A structure selection report should state the chosen type, alternatives, and the:','["slogan", "color", "justification", "joke"]'::jsonb,2,'Reports explain choices.','original'),
('127.414.d.14.B',4,'Justifying a structural choice includes explaining how it meets requirements for weight, strength, and:','["color", "name", "mood", "cost"]'::jsonb,3,'Justification uses multiple criteria.','original'),
('127.414.d.15.A',3,'Dead reckoning estimates an aircraft''s position from its known starting point, heading, speed, and:','["elapsed time", "weather forecast only", "altitude of birds", "color"]'::jsonb,0,'Distance = speed x time.','original'),
('127.414.d.15.A',4,'An aircraft flies at 120 knots on a heading of 090 for 30 minutes. It travels about:','["30 nautical miles", "60 nautical miles", "120 nautical miles", "15 nautical miles"]'::jsonb,1,'120 x 0.5 = 60.','original'),
('127.414.d.15.B',3,'An Automatic Direction Finder points toward a ground-based radio:','["cloud", "bird", "beacon", "mountain"]'::jsonb,2,'ADF tracks radio signals.','original'),
('127.414.d.15.B',4,'VOR navigation provides a pilot with a bearing or radial relative to a:','["cloud", "satellite only", "another aircraft only", "ground station"]'::jsonb,3,'VOR uses ground stations.','original'),
('127.414.d.15.C',3,'An Inertial Navigation System determines position by sensing the aircraft''s:','["accelerations and rotations", "star patterns only", "magnetic poles only", "radio beacons"]'::jsonb,0,'INS integrates motion sensors.','original'),
('127.414.d.15.C',4,'A key advantage of an INS is that it works without needing:','["power", "external radio signals", "sensors", "a computer"]'::jsonb,1,'INS is self-contained.','original'),
('127.414.d.15.D',3,'GPS determines position by measuring signals from several:','["buildings", "clouds", "satellites", "airports only"]'::jsonb,2,'GPS uses satellite ranging.','original'),
('127.414.d.15.D',4,'A GPS receiver needs signals from at least four satellites to compute a three-dimensional position and the receiver''s:','["mass", "name", "color", "clock error"]'::jsonb,3,'The fourth satellite corrects the clock.','original'),
('127.415.d.1.A',3,'A civil engineering firm starts a bridge replacement by first writing a clear statement of the:','["problem to be solved", "contractor''s lunch", "cost of paint", "firm''s logo"]'::jsonb,0,'A clear problem definition guides the work.','original'),
('127.415.d.1.A',4,'Defining the problem for a flood-control project early helps the team avoid designing a solution for the wrong:','["color", "need", "mascot", "name"]'::jsonb,1,'The right problem leads to the right design.','original'),
('127.415.d.1.B',3,'Before designing a culvert, an engineer researches rainfall records and:','["lunch menus", "fashion trends", "existing drainage conditions", "team songs"]'::jsonb,2,'Research informs design.','original'),
('127.415.d.1.B',4,'Studying how similar retaining walls have performed over time is part of the:','["marketing stage", "testing stage", "closing stage", "research stage"]'::jsonb,3,'Past performance informs new designs.','original'),
('127.415.d.1.C',3,'Ideation for a city park drainage problem involves listing many possible:','["solutions", "rules", "deadlines", "penalties"]'::jsonb,0,'Ideation produces options.','original'),
('127.415.d.1.C',4,'Conceptualization turns a drainage idea, like a rain garden, into a rough design that can be:','["planted at once", "compared with other concepts", "forgotten", "painted"]'::jsonb,1,'Concepts are early designs.','original'),
('127.415.d.1.D',3,'A team chooses between three bridge concepts by scoring each against cost, safety, and:','["color", "slogan", "constructability", "mascot"]'::jsonb,2,'Criteria drive selection.','original'),
('127.415.d.1.D',4,'Selecting a concept for detailed development means choosing the option that best satisfies the project:','["fashions", "moods", "jokes", "requirements"]'::jsonb,3,'Requirements guide selection.','original'),
('127.415.d.1.E',3,'A site plan that shows buildings and roads at an exact scale is a:','["technical drawing", "poem", "diary", "cartoon"]'::jsonb,0,'Technical drawings use scale and standards.','original'),
('127.415.d.1.E',4,'A small physical model of a dam spillway used to see how water flows is a:','["budget", "scale model", "contract", "permit"]'::jsonb,1,'Models test concepts.','original'),
('127.415.d.1.F',3,'To test the strength of a new concrete mix, a lab breaks several cylinders and records the:','["color", "price", "load at failure", "name"]'::jsonb,2,'Tests give data.','original'),
('127.415.d.1.F',4,'Testing several cylinders instead of one improves confidence because it reduces the effect of:','["requirements", "design", "gravity", "random variation"]'::jsonb,3,'Repetition improves reliability.','original'),
('127.415.d.1.G',3,'After a test shows a footing settles too much, the engineers revise the footing design and:','["test it again", "hide the result", "ignore it", "stop"]'::jsonb,0,'Iteration follows test results.','original'),
('127.415.d.1.G',4,'Design is iterative in civil projects because new site information often requires revisiting earlier:','["accidents", "design decisions", "bills", "lunches"]'::jsonb,1,'New data prompts revisions.','original'),
('127.415.d.1.H',3,'Presenting a stormwater design to a city council requires communicating clearly in drawings and:','["jargon only", "jokes", "plain language", "silence"]'::jsonb,2,'Clear communication supports approval.','original'),
('127.415.d.1.H',4,'Effective communication of a civil design helps ensure the contractor builds what the engineer:','["hid", "ignored", "forgot", "intended"]'::jsonb,3,'Clear documents prevent errors.','original'),
('127.415.d.2.A',3,'A civil project manager keeps a long project on time by setting interim deadlines and ranking tasks by:','["priority", "author", "size of font", "color"]'::jsonb,0,'Priorities guide the schedule.','original'),
('127.415.d.2.A',4,'Doing a task that other tasks depend on first helps prevent:','["early finish", "delays downstream", "good progress", "rework"]'::jsonb,1,'Dependencies drive priority.','original'),
('127.415.d.2.B',3,'For a highway project, a limit on available right-of-way is a:','["hobby", "bonus", "constraint", "feature"]'::jsonb,2,'Constraints bound the design.','original'),
('127.415.d.2.B',4,'Planning around a seasonal weather constraint, such as winter, means scheduling concrete placement for:','["only at night", "any time", "freezing days", "favorable conditions"]'::jsonb,3,'Constraints shape the schedule.','original'),
('127.415.d.2.C',3,'The completion of a foundation is a project:','["milestone", "hobby", "mistake", "complaint"]'::jsonb,0,'Milestones mark progress.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
