from (values
('127.415.d.2.C',4,'Dividing a large project into stages with deliverables helps managers see problems:','["later", "earlier", "only at the end", "never"]'::jsonb,1,'Stages enable tracking.','original'),
('127.415.d.2.D',3,'An email to a surveyor asking for a quick elevation check is an example of:','["a formal report", "a permit", "informal written communication", "a legal contract"]'::jsonb,2,'Different types suit different needs.','original'),
('127.415.d.2.D',4,'Stamped engineering drawings issued to a contractor are an example of:','["guess", "rumor", "casual chat", "formal communication"]'::jsonb,3,'Formal documents carry authority.','original'),
('127.415.d.2.E',3,'When a site plan does not fit the property, a good problem-solving strategy is to break the problem into:','["smaller pieces", "jokes", "rumors", "bigger problems"]'::jsonb,0,'Decomposition simplifies problems.','original'),
('127.415.d.2.E',4,'Problem solving on a construction team works best when members describe the problem and propose:','["blame", "tested alternatives", "delays", "excuses"]'::jsonb,1,'Collaboration finds solutions.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.A',3,'Under workplace safety laws, employers are responsible for providing a workplace that is free of:','["coworkers", "paperwork", "recognized hazards", "schedules"]'::jsonb,2,'Employers must protect workers.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.A',4,'Workers have a responsibility to follow safety rules and to report:','["good weather", "coworker names", "lunch breaks", "unsafe conditions"]'::jsonb,3,'Safety is shared.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.B',3,'OSHA stands for the Occupational Safety and Health:','["Administration", "Association", "Agency of Texas", "Alliance"]'::jsonb,0,'OSHA is the federal agency.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.B',4,'OSHA standards are important because they set minimum requirements to protect workers from:','["paying taxes", "workplace hazards", "taking breaks", "being hired"]'::jsonb,1,'Standards reduce injuries.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.C',3,'Industrial hygiene focuses on recognizing, evaluating, and controlling workplace:','["staff lunches", "office layouts", "health hazards like dust and noise", "paint choices"]'::jsonb,2,'Industrial hygiene protects health.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.C',4,'Measuring dust levels on a demolition site is an industrial hygiene activity that helps protect workers'' long-term:','["wages", "vacations", "hours", "health"]'::jsonb,3,'Exposure monitoring protects health.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.D',3,'Hard hats, safety glasses, and gloves are examples of:','["personal protective equipment", "signage only", "engineering controls only", "work rules"]'::jsonb,0,'PPE protects the wearer.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.D',4,'Respirators are needed on a site when airborne contaminants cannot be:','["named", "controlled by other means", "smelled", "seen"]'::jsonb,1,'PPE is the last line of defense.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.E',3,'Slippery, uneven, or cluttered floors create a risk of:','["gas leaks", "fires only", "slips, trips, and falls", "noise"]'::jsonb,2,'Surfaces must be safe.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.E',4,'Guardrails or fall protection are needed when working at heights because falls are a leading cause of:','["paperwork", "good weather", "delays only", "construction deaths"]'::jsonb,3,'Fall protection saves lives.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.F',3,'Using a damaged extension cord on a job site creates a risk of:','["electric shock", "tripping only", "sunburn", "noise"]'::jsonb,0,'Damaged cords are shock hazards.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.F',4,'Digging near buried power lines without locating them first risks:','["sunburn", "electrocution", "noise only", "boredom"]'::jsonb,1,'Utility locates prevent contact.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.G',3,'A safety data sheet for a chemical includes information on its hazards and:','["price", "color", "safe handling and storage", "advertising"]'::jsonb,2,'SDSs guide safe use.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.G',4,'Flammable liquids should be stored in approved containers away from:','["tools", "workers'' lunches", "shade", "ignition sources"]'::jsonb,3,'Separation prevents fires.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.H',3,'Posted exit routes and a map of emergency equipment are examples of workplace:','["safety resources", "games", "decorations", "advertising"]'::jsonb,0,'Resources help in emergencies.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.H',4,'A workplace emergency plan helps workers know where to go and whom to:','["avoid", "contact", "blame", "ignore"]'::jsonb,1,'Plans guide response.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.I',3,'Management leadership, worker participation, and hazard identification are elements of a:','["marketing plan", "uniform policy", "safety and health program", "payroll process"]'::jsonb,2,'Programs organize safety efforts.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.I',4,'Including workers in safety planning improves a safety program because workers know the:','["office color", "vendor list", "company logo", "hazards of their daily tasks"]'::jsonb,3,'Worker input improves programs.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.J',3,'A written emergency action plan describes how to report emergencies and how to:','["evacuate", "paint", "advertise", "hire"]'::jsonb,0,'Plans give clear steps.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.J',4,'Written plans and regular drills are important because people act more safely in an emergency when they have:','["never heard of it", "practiced the plan", "forgotten it", "ignored it"]'::jsonb,1,'Practice improves response.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.K',3,'A hazard communication program requires labels, safety data sheets, and worker:','["uniforms", "bonuses", "training", "vacations"]'::jsonb,2,'Programs inform workers about chemicals.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.K',4,'A container label that shows a flame pictogram warns workers that the contents are:','["edible", "cold", "heavy", "flammable"]'::jsonb,3,'Pictograms communicate hazards.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.L',3,'Workers must receive training on the hazards of their jobs and how to:','["protect themselves", "avoid work", "hide errors", "skip breaks"]'::jsonb,0,'Training prevents injuries.','original'),
('127.415.d.3.L',4,'Training for a new employee on a construction site should happen before the worker is exposed to the:','["paperwork", "hazards", "coffee", "office"]'::jsonb,1,'Training must come first.','original'),
('127.415.d.4.A',3,'To become a licensed professional engineer, a candidate typically first passes the Fundamentals of Engineering exam and becomes an:','["surveyor only", "inspector", "engineer in training", "contractor"]'::jsonb,2,'EIT is the first step.','original'),
('127.415.d.4.A',4,'After gaining required experience, an engineer in training may take the Principles and Practice exam to earn a:','["driver''s license", "trade permit", "pilot license", "professional engineer license"]'::jsonb,3,'The PE exam follows experience.','original'),
('127.415.d.4.B',3,'Civil engineering careers include structural engineer, transportation engineer, and:','["geotechnical engineer", "poet", "pastry chef", "DJ"]'::jsonb,0,'Geotechnical engineers study soil.','original'),
('127.415.d.4.B',4,'An engineer who designs highways and traffic signals works in the sub-discipline of:','["aerospace engineering", "transportation engineering", "chemical engineering", "electrical engineering only"]'::jsonb,1,'Transportation engineers design roads.','original'),
('127.415.d.4.C',3,'A professional credential in engineering shows that a person has met standards of:','["luck", "wealth", "education, experience, and examination", "popularity"]'::jsonb,2,'Credentials show qualification.','original'),
('127.415.d.4.C',4,'Many states require continuing education to keep a professional engineering license:','["cheap", "blank", "secret", "current"]'::jsonb,3,'Licenses must be renewed.','original'),
('127.415.d.4.D',3,'Hydrology, flood control, and reservoir design belong to the civil sub-discipline concerned with the movement and control of:','["water", "electricity only", "traffic only", "music"]'::jsonb,0,'Water resources covers flow and storage.','original'),
('127.415.d.4.D',4,'An engineer who designs foundations and studies soil behavior is a:','["electrical engineer", "geotechnical engineer", "structural painter", "transportation engineer"]'::jsonb,1,'Geotechnical engineers focus on soil.','original'),
('127.415.d.5.A',3,'The mean of the data set 4, 6, 8, 10, 12 is:','["40", "10", "8", "6"]'::jsonb,2,'Sum 40 divided by 5 = 8.','original'),
('127.415.d.5.A',4,'For the data set 3, 5, 5, 7, 20, the median is:','["7", "20", "8", "5"]'::jsonb,3,'The middle value is 5.','original'),
('127.415.d.5.B',3,'Standard deviation measures how spread out data values are from the:','["mean", "minimum", "range only", "maximum"]'::jsonb,0,'It measures dispersion.','original'),
('127.415.d.5.B',4,'A data set with a smaller standard deviation has values that are:','["negative", "closer together", "all equal always", "farther apart"]'::jsonb,1,'Smaller SD means less spread.','original'),
('127.415.d.5.C',3,'The highest point of a normal distribution curve is located at the:','["edge", "tail", "mean", "minimum"]'::jsonb,2,'The peak is at the mean.','original'),
('127.415.d.5.C',4,'A normal distribution curve is symmetric about its:','["tail", "edge", "origin only", "center"]'::jsonb,3,'It is bell-shaped and symmetric.','original'),
('127.415.d.5.D',3,'The Empirical Rule states that about 68 percent of values lie within one standard deviation of the:','["mean", "range", "maximum", "minimum"]'::jsonb,0,'68-95-99.7.','original'),
('127.415.d.5.D',4,'According to the Empirical Rule, about what percent of data fall within two standard deviations of the mean?','["68 percent", "95 percent", "99.7 percent", "50 percent"]'::jsonb,1,'68-95-99.7.','original'),
('127.415.d.5.E',3,'A survey instrument that always reads 2 mm too high has a:','["no error", "gross error", "systematic error", "random error"]'::jsonb,2,'Consistent bias is systematic.','original'),
('127.415.d.5.E',4,'Reading a measurement wrongly by a large amount because of a mistake is called a:','["systematic error", "tolerance", "random error", "gross error"]'::jsonb,3,'Gross errors are blunders.','original'),
('127.415.d.5.F',3,'Accuracy describes how close a measurement is to the:','["true value", "largest value", "previous reading", "average of nothing"]'::jsonb,0,'Accuracy is closeness to truth.','original'),
('127.415.d.5.F',4,'Precision describes how close repeated measurements are to each other, regardless of the:','["time", "true value", "operator", "tool"]'::jsonb,1,'Precision is repeatability.','original'),
('127.415.d.5.G',3,'Measurements of 10.01, 10.02, and 10.01 m for a true length of 12.00 m are precise but not:','["measured", "close", "accurate", "repeated"]'::jsonb,2,'Values cluster but miss the true value.','original'),
('127.415.d.5.G',4,'Readings of 11.5, 12.4, and 12.0 m for a true length of 12.00 m average near the truth but are:','["very precise", "perfect", "identical", "not very precise"]'::jsonb,3,'Readings are widely spread.','original'),
('127.415.d.5.H',3,'A polygon with four equal sides and four right angles is a:','["square", "circle", "pentagon", "triangle"]'::jsonb,0,'Squares have equal sides and right angles.','original'),
('127.415.d.5.H',4,'The sum of the interior angles of any triangle is:','["270 degrees", "180 degrees", "360 degrees", "90 degrees"]'::jsonb,1,'Triangle angles sum to 180.','original'),
('127.415.d.5.I',3,'A right triangle has legs 3 m and 4 m. The hypotenuse is:','["7 m", "1 m", "5 m", "12 m"]'::jsonb,2,'3^2 + 4^2 = 25.','original'),
('127.415.d.5.I',4,'The law of sines relates the sides of any triangle to the sines of their opposite:','["areas", "sides", "colors", "angles"]'::jsonb,3,'a/sin A = b/sin B.','original')
